                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_interpret:     `<strong>Good — you started with yourself.</strong> Naming where your decisive, low-disclosure style reads as cold in this culture is the whole point. Now you can lead <em>consciously</em> instead of acting your defaults out unconsciously and triggering the resistance you're trying to avoid.`,
                    coach_r1_skim:          `<strong>You've half-read it and reached for the plan.</strong> Skimming your type isn't self-awareness. The plan isn't where this change is won or lost — <em>how you're experienced</em> by the division is, and that starts with knowing how your wiring will land here.`,
                    coach_r1_skip:          `<strong>“I know how I lead” is the trap.</strong> Your instinct — push, facts, pace — is precisely the style that reads as cold and rushed in a 144-year relationship culture. You're about to lead authentically by leading unconsciously. What does knowing yourself tell you to do <em>differently</em> here?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp self-awareness.</strong> You interpreted the profile AND named the real gap: your default is push-facts-pace, but this culture needs disclosure, patience and visible empathy. That is the foundation that lets every later choice land as authentic rather than instinctive.`,
                    coach_r1_drive:         `<strong>Dialling up drive and decisiveness is dialling up your default.</strong> That's not what authentic leadership means here — it's the opposite. This change needs the EI behaviours your type under-uses: empathy and self-regulation, not more push.`,
                    coach_r1_charisma:      `<strong>Optimism and charisma sell a vision, but they don't close the trust gap.</strong> In a culture grieving 180 reshaped roles, energy without empathy reads as not listening. Dial up the behaviours your default under-uses.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcommit:     `<strong>Low commitment to leading consciously means you'll revert to instinct under pressure.</strong> Authentic leadership is disciplined, not occasional. If you don't commit now, your defaults will run the launch for you.`,
                    coach_r2_loss:          `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> Her resistance is loss and fear for her people — a values response from the cultural anchor, not obstruction. Diagnosed that way, it tells you exactly what she needs: to be heard and brought into the design, not commanded.`,
                    coach_r2_grow:          `<strong>Coaching her in is how an anchor becomes an advocate.</strong> A GROW conversation that listens, reframes and co-opts her into designing the reskilling gives her agency and reads her resistance as information. That's the one move that can carry ~60% of the division with you.`,
                    coach_r2_ego:           `<strong>Reading her as ego misreads the anchor.</strong> Twenty-two years and 140 people don't resist out of territory — they resist out of loss and care. Misdiagnose it and your intervention will land wrong, however well-meant.`,
                    coach_r2_obstruction:   `<strong>Calling her an obstruction is the costly mistake.</strong> Treat the cultural anchor as a blocker to manage around and you confirm her worst fear and hand 60% of the division a reason to follow her out. Her resistance is information, not insubordination.`,
                    coach_r2_direct:        `<strong>You told your most respected manager to get on board. She nodded and went quiet.</strong> Quiet isn't agreement — it's resistance gone underground, and 60% of the division reads her body language better than your memo. You had the one person who could carry this, and you just turned an anchor into a saboteur.`,
                    coach_r2_transact:      `<strong>A bonus and a title buys her compliance, not belief.</strong> Transactional deals work on transactional people; the cultural anchor isn't one. She'll take it and still telegraph her doubt to the 140. Coach her in — don't buy her off.`,
                    coach_r2_overpromise:   `<strong>Telling her no one will be affected wins today and detonates later.</strong> You can't keep that promise — 180 roles are reshaped. When the reality lands, you're not just changing the firm, you're the leader who wasn't straight with her. In a trust culture, that is the fatal move.`,
                    coach_r2_vague:         `<strong>Warm noises and no commitment leave her exactly where she was.</strong> The anchor can smell vagueness. Authentic means committing to what you can — the reskilling fight, a voice in the design — and being honest about what you can't.`,
                    coach_r3_lossrenewal:   `<strong>Loss-and-renewal fits this culture.</strong> Naming honestly what ends, then the member-purpose it serves, honours Bridges' endings before new beginnings. It treats 600 people as adults who can hold the hard truth and the hope together.`,
                    coach_r3_opportunity:   `<strong>Selling it as pure opportunity skips the grief.</strong> To people losing roles and branches, “exciting career step” reads as not listening. You can name the opportunity — but only after you've honoured what's being lost.`,
                    coach_r3_overreassure:  `<strong>You've soft-pedalled the closures to protect trust.</strong> “No one needs to worry” feels kind now — but the closure list will leak, and then you're the leader who wasn't straight with them. In a trust culture that's fatal. How much hard truth should you have led with?`,
                    coach_r3_brutal:        `<strong>Leading with almost pure hard truth and no reassurance is candour without care.</strong> In this culture, all facts and no empathy reads as cold and lands as a threat, not honesty. Lead with the truth — but pair it with the renewal and the reskilling commitment.`,
                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `<strong>That's disciplined authenticity.</strong> True to your direct style, but consciously empathetic and disclosing — so a loss-and-renewal frame lands as genuine rather than scripted. You stayed yourself <em>and</em> dialled up the EI this moment needed.`,
                    coach_r3_scripted:      `<strong>A polished, scripted speech that hides your discomfort lands as scripted.</strong> People in a trust culture can tell. Authentic isn't a Comms-written performance — it's your real voice, disciplined to be empathetic. Owning your own words, imperfect, beats a flawless script.`,
                    coach_r3_unfiltered:    `<strong>“I'm just being authentic” is an EI failure with a noble label.</strong> Being blunt about 180 reshaped roles with no empathy isn't honesty — it's venting your defaults. Authentic leadership is self-aware, value-consistent behaviour. Stay true to yourself <em>and</em> dial up the empathy this moment needs.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>Owning the leak fast is the trust-protective move.</strong> Confirming the facts, holding the frame and explaining the reskilling commitment openly turns a breach into a demonstration that you're straight with people. That's how candour buys trust back.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>Calling the leaked list “speculation” is the move that ends trust.</strong> They've seen the list. Deny what they can read and you're no longer just changing the firm — you're the leader who wasn't honest, in the one culture where that can't be recovered cheaply.`,
                    coach_r4_clampdown:     `<strong>Hunting the leaker tells the division exactly who you are under pressure.</strong> Controlling the message instead of being straight confirms every fear the anchor voiced. You'll spend trust you can't get back chasing a leak that's already public.`,
                    coach_r4_invest_full:   `<strong>That's the visible “we look after our own” move.</strong> Committing the full envelope to reskilling and redeployment, right after owning the leak, proves the 144-year promise with money, not words. This is the single action that most moves adoption and trust together.`,
                    coach_r4_underfund:     `<strong>You trimmed reskilling to hit a number, so some of the 180 are let go.</strong> In 144 years this mutual has never made a compulsory redundancy. You've just spent the firm's deepest cultural promise to save a few hundred thousand — and every remaining employee now knows the “we look after our own” story is over. Was it worth the soul of the place?`,
                    coach_r4_sideline:      `<strong>Routing the change around the anchor confirms her doubt.</strong> She's still watching, and so are her 140. Sideline her now and you waste the one relationship that could carry adoption — and hand the doubters their leader.`,
                    coach_r4_announce:      `<strong>“Change communicated” is not change adopted.</strong> Your all-hands had applause and good energy, but the board measures behavioural take-up — and weeks later people quietly keep the old way. You announced a change; you didn't lead one. Where's the adoption?`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Spinning the number in front of the board is the short game.</strong> They'll see the resistance you downplayed the moment it surfaces, and then you've lost their trust as well as the division's. An honest, evidenced read is the only one that survives contact with reality.`,
                    coach_r4_leak_hidden:   `<strong>The hard truth you hid in your launch just leaked.</strong> The soft-pedalled closures are now public, and in a trust culture the leader who wasn't straight pays in collapsing trust — exactly the asset the whole change runs on. Candour up front would have cost less than this.`,
                    coach_r4_leak_overpromise: `<strong>The promise you made the anchor — “no one will be affected” — just met reality.</strong> The closure list is public and her people are on it. You're now the leader who wasn't straight with the one person who could carry the division, and the trust cost is severe.`,

